🔥 25 Web API Interview-Based Q&A — ASP.NET MVC
1. What is Web API?

Answer:

ASP.NET Web API is a framework used to build HTTP-based services that allow different applications to communicate with each other.

For example:

React / Angular / Mobile App
          ↓
       HTTP Request
          ↓
      ASP.NET Web API
          ↓
       Repository
          ↓
       Database

The client can send requests such as:

GET    /api/employees
POST   /api/employees
PUT    /api/employees/10
DELETE /api/employees/10

The API generally returns data in JSON format.

2. What is the difference between MVC and Web API?

Answer:

The main difference is their purpose.

MVC	Web API
Mainly used to build web applications	Mainly used to build HTTP services/APIs
Returns Views	Usually returns JSON/XML
Uses Controllers + Views	Uses API Controllers
Browser-oriented	Client/application-oriented
Controller	ApiController

Example MVC:

public ActionResult Index()
{
    return View();
}

Web API:

public IHttpActionResult GetEmployees()
{
    return Ok(employees);
}

So:

MVC → UI
Web API → Data/Services
3. What is ApiController?

Answer:

ApiController is the base class used for creating ASP.NET Web API controllers.

Example:

public class EmployeeController : ApiController
{
    public IHttpActionResult GetEmployees()
    {
        return Ok();
    }
}

It provides functionality specifically designed for HTTP APIs.

For example:

HTTP status codes
Content negotiation
Request handling
Routing
JSON/XML responses
4. What is the difference between Controller and ApiController?

Answer:

MVC controller:

public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    public ActionResult Index()
    {
        return View();
    }
}

API controller:

public class EmployeeController : ApiController
{
    public IHttpActionResult Get()
    {
        return Ok();
    }
}

Controller is normally used when returning HTML Views.

ApiController is used when returning HTTP data, usually JSON.

5. What are HTTP methods in Web API?

Answer:

The major HTTP methods are:

Method	Purpose
GET	Retrieve data
POST	Create data
PUT	Update/replace data
PATCH	Partially update data
DELETE	Delete data

For example:

GET /api/employees

gets employees.

POST /api/employees

creates an employee.

PUT /api/employees/5

updates employee 5.

DELETE /api/employees/5

deletes employee 5.

6. What is REST API?

Answer:

REST stands for Representational State Transfer.

A REST API uses HTTP methods and resources to communicate.

For example:

/api/employees

represents the employee resource.

Then:

GET /api/employees

gets all employees.

GET /api/employees/5

gets employee 5.

POST /api/employees

creates an employee.

DELETE /api/employees/5

deletes employee 5.

A good REST API is generally:

Stateless
Resource-oriented
HTTP-based
Uses appropriate HTTP methods
Uses HTTP status codes
7. What does stateless mean in REST?

Answer:

Stateless means that the server doesn't depend on information stored from a previous request to understand the current request.

For example:

GET /api/employees/10
Authorization: Bearer token

The request contains everything required to authenticate and process it.

The server doesn't have to remember:

"This user made a request 5 minutes ago, so I'll assume they are authenticated."

Each request should contain the necessary information.

8. What is routing in Web API?

Answer:

Routing determines which controller/action should handle an incoming HTTP request.

For example:

GET /api/employees/10

could be mapped to:

public IHttpActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
{
    ...
}

In Web API 2, routing can be configured using conventional routing:

config.Routes.MapHttpRoute(
    name: "DefaultApi",
    routeTemplate: "api/{controller}/{id}",
    defaults: new { id = RouteParameter.Optional }
);

Or attribute routing:

[Route("api/employees/{id}")]
public IHttpActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
{
    ...
}
9. What is attribute routing?

Answer:

Attribute routing allows us to define the URL directly above the API action.

Example:

[Route("api/employees/{id}")]
[HttpGet]
public IHttpActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
{
    ...
}

Request:

GET /api/employees/10

will call this method.

Usually we enable it in Web API configuration:

config.MapHttpAttributeRoutes();
10. What is IHttpActionResult?

Answer:

IHttpActionResult represents the HTTP response returned by a Web API action.

For example:

public IHttpActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
{
    var employee = repository.GetById(id);

    if (employee == null)
        return NotFound();

    return Ok(employee);
}

Here:

Ok(employee)

returns HTTP 200.

NotFound()

returns HTTP 404.

This makes the API code cleaner than manually creating every response.

11. What are HTTP status codes and why are they important?

Answer:

HTTP status codes tell the client what happened.

Common ones are:

200 → OK
201 → Created
204 → No Content
400 → Bad Request
401 → Unauthorized
403 → Forbidden
404 → Not Found
500 → Internal Server Error

For example:

return Ok(employee);

means:

200 OK

And:

return NotFound();

means:

404 Not Found

An interviewer may ask:

Why shouldn't every API return HTTP 200?

Because the client needs to know whether the operation actually succeeded or failed.

12. What is the difference between 401 and 403?

Answer:

This is a very common interview question.

401 — Unauthorized

The user has not been properly authenticated.

Example:

No valid authentication token
403 — Forbidden

The user is authenticated but doesn't have permission.

Example:

User is logged in
BUT
User doesn't have Admin permission

Think:

401 → Who are you?
403 → I know who you are, but you can't do this.
13. What is the difference between POST and PUT?

Answer:

POST is generally used to create a new resource.

POST /api/employees

For example:

{
    "name": "John",
    "email": "john@test.com"
}

PUT is generally used to update/replace an existing resource.

PUT /api/employees/10

with:

{
    "name": "John Updated",
    "email": "johnnew@test.com"
}

Simple way to remember:

POST → Create
PUT → Update/replace
14. What is PATCH?

Answer:

PATCH is used when you want to update part of a resource.

Suppose employee has:

{
    "name": "John",
    "email": "john@test.com",
    "department": "IT"
}

If you only want to change the department:

PATCH /api/employees/10

you might send only:

{
    "department": "HR"
}

So:

PUT   → generally complete replacement
PATCH → partial modification
15. How do you receive JSON data in Web API?

Answer:

Suppose the client sends:

{
    "name": "John",
    "email": "john@test.com"
}

We can create a model:

public class Employee
{
    public int EmployeeId { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
}

Then:

[HttpPost]
public IHttpActionResult CreateEmployee(Employee employee)
{
    repository.Insert(employee);

    return Ok(employee);
}

Web API's model binding/deserialization converts the incoming JSON into the Employee object.

16. What is model binding?

Answer:

Model binding converts incoming HTTP request data into C# objects/parameters.

For example:

{
    "name": "John",
    "email": "john@test.com"
}

can become:

Employee employee

inside:

public IHttpActionResult Create(Employee employee)

So instead of manually reading every JSON property, Web API does the mapping for us.

17. What is content negotiation?

Answer:

Content negotiation determines what format the API should return based on the client's request and available formatters.

For example, a client can request:

Accept: application/json

and the API can return:

{
    "id": 10,
    "name": "John"
}

Web API can support formats such as:

JSON
XML

In modern applications, JSON is by far the most common.

18. What is CORS?

Answer:

CORS means:

Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

Suppose your frontend is running at:

http://localhost:3000

and your Web API is running at:

http://localhost:5000

These are different origins.

The browser may block the frontend from calling the API unless the API allows that origin through CORS.

For example:

React
localhost:3000
      ↓
      ↓ API request
      ↓
ASP.NET Web API
localhost:5000

CORS controls whether that cross-origin request is allowed.

This is especially important when you're integrating React + ASP.NET Web API.

19. How do you handle exceptions in Web API?

Answer:

We should not expose raw exceptions to clients.

For example, instead of:

try
{
    ...
}
catch(Exception ex)
{
    return InternalServerError(ex);
}

for every action, larger applications can use centralized exception handling/logging.

For a simple API:

try
{
    var employee = repository.GetById(id);

    return Ok(employee);
}
catch(Exception ex)
{
    // Log exception
    return InternalServerError();
}

The important point is:

Log the actual exception internally, but don't expose sensitive server/database details to the client.

20. What is dependency injection in Web API?

Answer:

Dependency Injection means that a controller receives the dependencies it needs instead of creating them itself.

Bad:

public EmployeeController()
{
    repository = new EmployeeRepository();
}

Better:

private readonly IEmployeeRepository _repository;

public EmployeeController(IEmployeeRepository repository)
{
    _repository = repository;
}

Then Unity can register:

container.RegisterType<IEmployeeRepository, EmployeeRepository>();

This is actually very relevant to your MVC project because you've worked with Unity DI + repositories.

The flow becomes:

API Controller
      ↓
IEmployeeRepository
      ↓
EmployeeRepository
      ↓
DbContext
      ↓
Database
21. What is the purpose of a repository in Web API?

Answer:

A repository separates database access logic from the controller.

Instead of doing everything inside the controller:

public IHttpActionResult GetEmployees()
{
    // SQL/database logic
    // filtering
    // inserting
    // updating
}

we can have:

public IHttpActionResult GetEmployees()
{
    var employees = _employeeRepository.GetAll();

    return Ok(employees);
}

The repository handles data access.

This creates separation of concerns:

Controller
   ↓
Repository
   ↓
Data Access
   ↓
Database
22. How would you create a CRUD Web API?

Answer:

A basic CRUD API could look like this:

public class EmployeeController : ApiController
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IHttpActionResult GetEmployees()
    {
        var employees = repository.GetAll();

        return Ok(employees);
    }

    [HttpGet]
    public IHttpActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
    {
        var employee = repository.GetByID(id);

        if (employee == null)
            return NotFound();

        return Ok(employee);
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IHttpActionResult Create(Employee employee)
    {
        repository.Insert(employee);

        return Ok(employee);
    }

    [HttpPut]
    public IHttpActionResult Update(int id, Employee employee)
    {
        repository.Update(employee);

        return Ok(employee);
    }

    [HttpDelete]
    public IHttpActionResult Delete(int id)
    {
        repository.Delete(id);

        return Ok();
    }
}

This provides:

GET     → Read
POST    → Create
PUT     → Update
DELETE  → Delete
23. Interview Scenario: Your API returns 500. How would you debug it?

Answer:

I wouldn't immediately assume the API code is wrong.

I'd debug systematically:

Step 1 — Check the request
URL
HTTP method
Request body
Headers
Step 2 — Check controller

Put a breakpoint inside the API action.

public IHttpActionResult Create(Employee employee)
{
    // breakpoint here
}
Step 3 — Check model binding

Check:

employee
ModelState
Step 4 — Check repository
_repository.Insert(employee);
Step 5 — Check database

Look for:

Invalid column
Constraint violation
NULL value
Foreign key violation
Connection problem
Step 6 — Check server logs

Look at the actual exception and stack trace.

A good interview answer is:

"I would trace the request from the client to the controller, then model binding, repository, database, and finally inspect the server-side exception/logs."

24. Interview Scenario: Your API is returning too much database data. What would you do?

Answer:

I wouldn't directly expose the database entity everywhere.

Instead, I would use a DTO — Data Transfer Object.

Suppose our database entity contains:

public class Employee
{
    public int EmployeeId { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
    public string Password { get; set; }
}

We should not return the password.

Create:

public class EmployeeDto
{
    public int EmployeeId { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
}

Then:

[HttpGet]
public IHttpActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
{
    var employee = repository.GetByID(id);

    var dto = new EmployeeDto
    {
        EmployeeId = employee.EmployeeId,
        Name = employee.Name,
        Email = employee.Email
    };

    return Ok(dto);
}

This provides better:

Security
Control over response
Separation between API and database models
Maintainability
25. 🔥 Interview Scenario: React calls your ASP.NET Web API but gets 404. What would you check?

Answer:

I'd check the following in order.

1. URL

For example:

axios.get("https://localhost:44300/api/employees")

Make sure the URL matches the API route.

2. Controller

Make sure the API controller exists:

public class EmployeeController : ApiController
3. Routing

Check:

config.MapHttpAttributeRoutes();

or the conventional route:

config.Routes.MapHttpRoute(
    name: "DefaultApi",
    routeTemplate: "api/{controller}/{id}",
    defaults: new { id = RouteParameter.Optional }
);
4. HTTP method

If the API expects:

[HttpGet]

the React application should send:

GET

not POST.

5. Browser Network tab

I'd inspect:

Request URL
Request Method
Status Code
Response
6. Test API separately

I'd test the API using Postman or Swagger if available.

If Postman works but React doesn't, the issue is likely in:

React URL
CORS
Headers
Request format
